/** \page decimater_docu Mesh Decimation Framework

  The mesh decimation framework has 3 building blocks.

  -# \ref DecimaterAlg
  -# \ref DecimaterMod
  -# \ref DecimaterHnd

\section DecimaterAlg The decimation algorithm

  The decimater (OpenMesh::Decimater::DecimaterT) provides the
  decimation algorithm, while the decimation modules provide the
  computational part. The modules compute a priority value due to some
  error metric, which is used by the decimater to feed a priority
  queue. The lower the error value, the more a potential collapse
  moves to the front of the queue. The one with the lowest error will
  always be the candidate for the next collapse.

  This implementation does a halfedge collapse, hence simply
  collapsing one vertex into another connected by a halfedge.

  \note The decimater ignores all 'locked' and 'deleted' vertices (see
  OpenMesh::Attributes::StatusBits) 

  \attention The decimater sets temporarily the status bit 'tagged' and
  clears it after usage regardless of a previous state.

\section DecimaterMod Decimating Modules

  The vertex to be removed is determined by a decimation module, which has
  to be derived from OpenMesh::Decimater::ModBaseT. The framework
  supplies already a few decimation modules. But it's very easy to build
  your own (\ref OpenMesh::Decimater::ModBaseT).  The most important
  function of a decimation module is
  OpenMesh::Decimater::ModBaseT::collapse_priority(). It takes an
  OpenMesh::Decimater::CollapseInfoT describing a potential halfedge
  collapse, and returns a value due to some error metric. The error
  value is used by the decimater to feed a priority queue. Collapses
  with low error will be executed first, and those with large error
  later. Of course a module computing the error quadric is provided
  (OpenMesh::Decimater::ModQuadricT).

  This framework allows to use more than one decimation module with
  some restrictions. Since the error value is always normalized and
  sometimes very difficult to compare to other metrics, the framework
  allows only one non-binary module, i.e. a module computing a float
  value. Every further module must be a binary module,
  i.e. collapse_prioerity() returns
  OpenMesh::Decimater::ModBaseT::LEGAL_COLLAPSE or
  OpenMesh::Decimater::ModBaseT::ILLEGAL_COLLAPSE. In the algorithm
  the binary modules are evaluated first. If the evaluated collapse
  passes the test, then the non-binary module contributes to the
  decision step.

  In some cases the module does not contribute anything to the
  decision engine of the decimater, but instead, e.g. simply collects
  information, while the decimater does it's work. For instance the
  module OpenMesh::Decimater::ModProgMeshT collects information from
  all collapses that have been done. This information can be used to
  generate progressive meshes as described in "Progressive meshes",
  Hoppe, 1996.
    
  Provided decimation modules(Binary: B, Continuous: C, Special: X):
 
  - OpenMesh::Decimater::ModAspectRatioT (B,C)
  - OpenMesh::Decimater::ModEdgeLengthT (B,C)
  - OpenMesh::Decimater::ModHausdorffT (B)
  - OpenMesh::Decimater::ModIndependentSetsT (B)
  - OpenMesh::Decimater::ModNormalDeviationT (B,C) 
  - OpenMesh::Decimater::ModNormalFlippingT (B)
  - OpenMesh::Decimater::ModProgMeshT (X)
  - OpenMesh::Decimater::ModQuadricT (B,C)
  - OpenMesh::Decimater::ModRoundnessT (B,C)

\section DecimaterHnd Module Handles

  Similar to properties the modules are represented outside the
  decimater by module handles. Before using the decimater a
  non-binary module must be registrated with the decimater.

  See \ref DecimaterExa.

\section DecimaterHistory Local, replayable collapse history

  OpenMesh::Decimater::DecimaterT can additionally keep a local,
  replayable history of the halfedge collapses it performs, so a modeling
  application can drag a simplification slider back and forth on the very
  same mesh object.  Unlike OpenMesh::Decimater::ModProgMeshT, which only
  stores vertex numbers for progressive mesh export, the history stores
  local before/after images of every affected slot, hence halfedge slots,
  per-corner attributes, status bits, boundary links, representative
  halfedges, points, normals, dynamic properties and the accumulated
  ModQuadricT quadrics can all be restored in place.

  The API on the decimater is:
  - <b>enable_history()</b>: starts a history session.  It only succeeds
    when the decimater is already initialized, no history is currently
    enabled on this decimater or its mesh, and the module setup is a
    non-binary OpenMesh::Decimater::ModQuadricT used alone or together
    with an OpenMesh::Decimater::ModNormalFlippingT.  Any other module
    combination is rejected with \c false and leaves the mesh and the
    modules untouched.
  - <b>disable_history()</b>: discards all records, releases the session
    and restores ordinary garbage collection behavior.  The destructor
    does the same.
  - <b>undo(n)</b> / <b>redo(n)</b>: move up to \p n records, returning
    the number actually moved; moving past either end simply stops, and
    \p n == 0 changes nothing.  Replay restores the stored images
    directly and never re-runs priorities, collapses, module callbacks or
    the transfer callback.
  - <b>history_size()</b>, <b>history_position()</b> and
    <b>history_stored_elements()</b>: const inspection of the current
    branch length, the number of applied records, and the number of
    individual property values retained across all records (one value of
    one property on one slot counts as one).

  Each record covers only the local support domain of its collapse (the
  two endpoints, their adjacent faces and all vertices, edges and
  halfedges of those faces).  No full-mesh or full-property-column copies
  are taken, the model is never serialized, and no shadow slots are
  appended to the mesh; the required storage therefore does not grow with
  unrelated, distant geometry.

  <b>Transfer callback.</b> Before enabling, an optional callback of type
  \code std::function<void(const CollapseInfo&)> \endcode can be
  registered with set_history_transfer().  It is invoked exactly once per
  recorded collapse, after module post-processing, and its effects are
  part of the stored after image (so undo/redo reproduce them without
  calling the callback again).  Through the mesh reference it captures,
  the callback transfers ordinary V/H/E/F properties onto the surviving
  elements.  It may write the slots adjacent to either endpoint before the
  collapse, including slots that become deleted.  It must not throw, must
  not change topology, points, status bits, normals or the property
  structure, must not touch module-private properties, and must not have
  external business side effects.

  <b>Usage protocol and preconditions.</b>
  -# Add all properties and modules.
  -# Call initialize().
  -# Optionally call set_history_transfer().
  -# Call enable_history().
  -# Drive decimation with decimate(), decimate_to() or
     decimate_to_faces(), and navigate with undo()/redo().
  -# Call disable_history() (or destroy the decimater) before using
     garbage collection.

  While a session is enabled the application must not edit the mesh
  topology, points, status bits or normals outside the decimater, must not
  edit property values or the property structure outside the transfer
  callback, must not add/remove modules, change module parameters or
  re-initialize, and must not replace the transfer callback.  Both
  garbage_collection() overloads throw std::logic_error while a session is
  enabled (an empty history included); compression works normally again
  after disable_history() or destruction.  The mesh must outlive the
  decimater, and at most one history session may be bound to a mesh at any
  time.  Decimating again from an undone position opens a new branch: the
  old future is discarded on the first successful collapse, while a call
  that performs no collapse never discards it.  Slots already deleted
  before the session are never resurrected by an undo.

  The legality check temporarily uses the vertices' \em tagged status bit;
  in history mode those temporary bits are always restored, so candidate
  probing never leaks tagged state to the caller.

  A complete example is in \ref DecimaterHistoryExa.

